WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.790
Welcome to your first Java lesson, where you'll

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:06.595
produce console output, the text a program

00:00:06.595 --> 00:00:09.802
displays, using statements, or instructions, and

00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:12.741
string literals, or text in quotation marks.

00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:16.110
Console output lets applications display

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.029
results, helps you find and fix problems by

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:22.084
showing what a program is doing, and supports

00:00:22.084 --> 00:00:25.275
command-line interfaces, where people work with

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:26.769
software through text.

00:00:27.624 --> 00:00:30.683
We'll make a short notice for students arriving

00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:32.114
at a campus help desk.

00:00:32.114 --> 00:00:35.238
We'll supply the wording; visitors won't need to

00:00:35.238 --> 00:00:36.149
type anything.

00:00:36.895 --> 00:00:40.069
The notice should greet each student and explain

00:00:40.069 --> 00:00:43.045
how to prepare, so they know what to do next.

00:00:43.091 --> 00:00:46.296
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:47.041
Main.java.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.013
Main.java is open.

00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:55.075
This supplied frame is our program's starter

00:00:55.075 --> 00:00:55.771
structure.

00:00:55.771 --> 00:00:58.416
Main is the class that holds our code.

00:00:58.416 --> 00:01:01.757
Its main method is a named group of instructions

00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:03.567
where this program starts.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:06.560
We'll put our message statements inside it.

00:01:12.297 --> 00:01:15.394
Those opening lines give our statements a place

00:01:15.394 --> 00:01:15.855
to run.

00:01:15.855 --> 00:01:18.887
Let's add one instruction, called a statement,

00:01:18.887 --> 00:01:20.666
to display a short welcome.

00:01:20.666 --> 00:01:23.697
Java's print-line instruction sends our string

00:01:23.697 --> 00:01:25.872
to the console and ends the line.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.549
Text written between quotation marks is a string

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:33.057
literal.

00:01:33.057 --> 00:01:36.042
The marks show its boundaries; they aren't part

00:01:36.042 --> 00:01:36.995
of the message.

00:01:36.995 --> 00:01:39.790
Parentheses hold the text, and the semicolon

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:40.997
ends the statement.

00:01:40.997 --> 00:01:43.855
Let's add a separate instruction for taking a

00:01:43.855 --> 00:01:44.300
number.

00:01:48.573 --> 00:01:50.734
We have two message statements.

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:53.800
Let's finish the supplied frame so the whole

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.473
program is ready to run.

00:01:57.481 --> 00:02:00.740
The completed frame contains both instructions.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.998
Let's click Run to compile and run the program.

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:06.911
Compilation checks Java's rules; execution

00:02:06.911 --> 00:02:08.921
carries out our instructions.

00:02:08.921 --> 00:02:11.556
Watch how the source becomes a notice.

00:02:16.734 --> 00:02:19.774
The console shows Welcome, then Please take a

00:02:19.774 --> 00:02:21.531
number, on separate lines.

00:02:21.531 --> 00:02:24.166
Execution followed the statement order.

00:02:24.166 --> 00:02:27.342
It printed the strings' text, without quotation

00:02:27.342 --> 00:02:29.166
marks or Java instructions.

00:02:29.166 --> 00:02:31.936
That gives us a pattern for the help desk

00:02:31.936 --> 00:02:32.409
notice.

00:02:32.758 --> 00:02:35.724
Let's replace only the two practice statements

00:02:35.724 --> 00:02:38.239
with the help desk's supplied messages.

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:41.012
We'll keep the working Main frame and use a

00:02:41.012 --> 00:02:43.333
separate statement for each message.

00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:57.907
The source now contains the help desk messages.

00:02:57.907 --> 00:03:00.754
Before we run it, write the exact two output

00:03:00.754 --> 00:03:01.724
lines in order.

00:03:01.724 --> 00:03:04.829
Will the quotation marks or the Java instruction

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:05.735
itself appear?

00:03:05.735 --> 00:03:08.128
Use what the practice run showed you.

00:03:08.128 --> 00:03:10.392
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:03:18.429 --> 00:03:21.654
Let's click Run to check your prediction.

00:03:25.836 --> 00:03:28.964
The console says Welcome to the help desk, then

00:03:28.964 --> 00:03:31.228
Please have your student ID ready.

00:03:31.228 --> 00:03:33.358
Both messages end with a period.

00:03:33.358 --> 00:03:36.220
They appear on separate lines, in statement

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:39.215
order, with no source-code punctuation added.

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:42.011
Compare that with the two lines you wrote.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.037
We've built the notice the help desk needed: a

00:03:45.037 --> 00:03:47.865
greeting followed by a preparation reminder.

00:03:47.865 --> 00:03:50.500
Strings preserved the wording, print-line

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.585
statements separated the messages, and statement

00:03:53.585 --> 00:03:55.963
order made the notice easy to follow.

00:03:55.963 --> 00:03:58.855
Compiling and running let us check the actual

00:03:58.855 --> 00:04:00.655
result, not just the source.

00:04:01.322 --> 00:04:03.923
Before returning to the notebook, imagine

00:04:03.923 --> 00:04:06.207
swapping the two message statements.

00:04:06.207 --> 00:04:08.999
What would change in the console, what would

00:04:08.999 --> 00:04:10.458
stay the same, and why?

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:13.376
Explain it using the difference between source

00:04:13.376 --> 00:04:14.772
code and printed text.
